# Who gets asked to look at a change, for every path in this tree.
#
# This file requests a review; it does not hold one back. The ruleset on master
# does not require a code owner's approval, and it requires no approval at all:
#
#     gh api repos/Flowfin/jellyfin-plugin-whisper-subtitles/rulesets/20467991 \
#       --jq '.rules[] | select(.type=="pull_request") | .parameters
#             | {require_code_owner_review, required_approving_review_count}'
#     {"require_code_owner_review":false,"required_approving_review_count":0}
#
# So what a reader gets from this file is a request on the pull request rather
# than a gate, and #54 is where the gate is decided. Saying that here is the
# point: a CODEOWNERS file reads like enforcement, and this one is not.
#
# One rule over every path rather than a split by directory. A per-directory
# split would be a claim about who maintains which part of this plugin, and
# writing a claim like that before it is true gives a reader the impression that
# a review request went somewhere it did not.
#
# An owner without write access is dropped silently, and a dropped owner is
# invisible on the pull request that should have named them. Read rather than
# assumed:
#
#     gh api repos/Flowfin/jellyfin-plugin-whisper-subtitles/collaborators \
#       --jq '.[] | "\(.login) \(.permissions.push)"'
#     iderex true

* @iderex
